      "claim": "Meptazinol absolute oral bioavailability in healthy volunteers is 4.4 ± 0.9%, below the 20% threshold.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Most high-transfer molecules with direct absolute oral bioavailability measurements support F >= 20%, including pethidine, SAHA, encainide, PEMA, FK788, tenidap, suprofen, R406, and ziprasidone. One high-transfer molecule, meptazinol, clearly supports low bioavailability. The preponderance of direct, ordinary-condition evidence favors high bioavailability.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "Molecule 1 includes a record (C34) referring to morphine, inconsistent with the other pethidine records; this provenance conflict was discounted.",
    "One high-transfer molecule (meptazinol) provides affirmative low-bioavailability evidence, leaving residual uncertainty."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

